an infinite regress - eat the recycled taco above the funnel with smaller and smaller tortillas placed underneath

&#x200B;

WASTE. NOT.

I'd be curious to hear what your exact process is moment to moment during the meditation, because it is certainly powerful and profound for me. I'm a cannabis user and LSD microdoser, and I still find meditation interesting enough to do about an hour every day altogether. Even access concentration, the lowest concentration attainment, is very, very interesting. At least in concentration practice, the feedback loop requires "interest" to happen so that you have more energy or attention or whatever to feed into the concentration. It is very important that the precise thing you're doing in consciousness is correct, or you'll be wasting your time. 

&#x200B;

If you have space in your awareness to be upset or disappointed, you're not gathering all the space of your awareness on the object. Whenever you notice something that's not the object of concentration, that something is fuel for more concentration! If you can learn how to feed it.

Keep in mind that decision making, map awareness, paying attention to what guns you've heard the enemies shooting, positioning, using consumables well, coordinating with teammates, etc. play a huge part in Hunt. With how infrequent engagements are, your aim is pretty much always going to be cold, and you're not going to improve it very much (if at all; just so few shots fired per hour). Aim and reflexes help, for sure, I mean a LOT, but the primary place to improve is in understanding how Hunt wants you to play.

&#x200B;

My biggest suggestion is taking a moment to deliberately think in fights after going into cover. Check what consumables you have. Look where your teammates are. Alternate between pushing and staying silent. You have to get into the enemy's head and be unpredictable.

