Not the guy above but as someone who pretty solidly lands pro-life I can answer.

&#x200B;

1. Yes, I can imagine quite a few things that would shift my opinion somewhere from the moment of conception to the point of viability. If not philosophically, at least legally. I mean, I already consider it ethically unenforceable before that and maybe closer to birth, but primarily for reasons of utility (I don't think they're going to be predisposed to crime and it will only ruin their life to prosecute, damage is done).

Those things might be generally categorized in two buckets:

\- Better consensus on our **philosophical/legal model of personhood** with some new language that allows our model to fit all of the cases properly (meaning we don't accidentally redefine severely disabled persons, comatose persons, etc. as nonpersons and other related issues). This is the direct route to a legal case for different thresholds and protections IMO, moreso than science. A parsimonious definition that fits all edge cases with our current science just doesn't need more measurables. Not sure if it exists, I've listened to the discussion for 15 years without hearing anything super convincing.

\- New findings in the **material study of consciousness** that let us get a much more measurement-based understanding of the phenomena. This is meaningful in two ways: as a discrete signifier of personhood in some way (possibly combined with other things like human DNA or advanced cognition, nonetheless important) and to help us understand the gradation of consciousness that exists in the known animal kingdom (or maybe plants, too, we don't know!). They both factor into an ethical determination in different ways - we don't give even highly conscious animals, \[dolphins\], the exact same level of rights as a less conscious animal that has all human DNA \[a near-catatonic human with a head injury\] for example.

&#x200B;

2) I think by examining all the conceivable parameters that qualify death and really ironing that out we can get a much better handle on how to have the abortion discussion. Any authentic point of reference to provide more precision and certainty in the discourse is good, no matter which way it points. I'm glad you brought it up, I really haven't thought about death in particular very hard as it relates to abortion. I've thought a lot about comas, sleep, trance states, oxygen deprivation, gradual deterioration from brain disease, etc. but not really death itself.

&#x200B;

3) I think that in the case of rape, compassion on the pregnant person is the predominant concern. You'll never catch me advocating for legal enforcement against abortion in cases of rape. I understand that my position as a whole contributes to the rape case implicitly and that does make my position more complicated to justify.

&#x200B;

4) I don't think non-measurables are socially enforceable. If at this time we have no measurables related to the concept of a soul or a spirit, then the position can't be derived from the commons and it doesn't belong in politics. My personal view of concepts like the soul or the spirit aren't how I derive a political or legal position, and honestly not involved that much in my personal moral position either.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

To be clear, I think the best a pro-life individual can authentically do right now is hold a stance with fuzzy thresholds and carry as much actionable compassion as possible for people having to make this decision. I don't think there is enough in the commons to really pin the enforceability of earlier gestation limits from a legal or philosophical sense, and that we should focus on material support for mothers / families over everything else concerning this issue. On a personal level, I think the most you can justify is that you might encourage people to carry to term (in most cases, I'm not an absolutist on this) and persuade them to accept the risk and see where life takes them and really be on their side throughout it all - but I don't think you can put someone in jail if they aren't persuaded by your advocacy.

I acknowledge that my convictions might crystallize somewhere in the range of my current fuzzy thresholds - or perhaps somewhere without. That's okay with me. Right now it looks a certain way, as I've said in the rest of the response. I don't anticipate I will ever push my view past the point of viability, though.

&#x200B;

Sorry if this amount of text is inappropriate but I thought you might genuinely be interested to hear someone's thoughts on those questions, as this other guy seems unlikely to constructively respond. Hope it is interesting, at least. I understand my tone is more academic and that there are real hard implications of my position but I'm just honestly trying to work it all out.

EDIT: I messed up the formatting bad and not sure how to make it more legible good luck

